# fs Module (Sync vs Async) ⭐⭐⭐☆☆

## 🤯 Problem

Your server needs to read a config file at startup.

100 other users are hitting the server RIGHT NOW.

If reading that file BLOCKS the whole thread,

every other user's request freezes too.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine the Waiter (Event Loop) again.

Async = He hands your food order to the kitchen and moves to the next table.

Sync = He personally walks to the kitchen, stands there, and waits — ignoring every other table until it's done.

You NEVER want a sync waiter in a busy restaurant.

---

## ✅ What is it?

`fs` is Node's built-in module to read/write files.

Async methods (`fs.readFile`) → non-blocking, use a callback.

Sync methods (`fs.readFileSync`) → blocking, stop everything until done.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

To read/write config files, logs, uploaded files, templates, etc.

Prefer ASYNC in a running server

so one slow file operation doesn't freeze everyone else.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🚫 Sync = Freeze the Restaurant

Sync methods have "Sync" in the name —

that's your warning sign: "this will block."

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

Generating a PDF Report Card

reads a HTML template file first.

Using `fs.readFile` (async)

↓

other teachers' requests keep processing normally

↓

once the template is read, PDF generation continues.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Using `fs.readFileSync` inside a running Express route handler.

This blocks the ENTIRE server for every user

while that one file is being read.

Sync versions are only okay at server STARTUP, not inside routes.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```js
const fs = require('fs');

// Async (preferred inside routes)
fs.readFile('data.txt', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
    if (err) throw err;
    console.log(data);
});

// Sync (only safe at startup, blocks the thread)
const data = fs.readFileSync('data.txt', 'utf8');
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Like the difference between

a synchronous function call

and an Observable/Promise in Angular services.

Async keeps the app responsive.

Sync locks everything up until it's done.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

The fs module in Node.js provides both synchronous and asynchronous methods for file operations. Asynchronous methods are non-blocking and use callbacks, allowing the event loop to keep processing other requests. Synchronous methods block the thread until the operation completes. In a running server, asynchronous methods are preferred to avoid blocking other users' requests.

---

## 📝 Keywords

fs module

readFile

readFileSync

Blocking

Non-blocking

---


# http Module (raw server) ⭐⭐⭐☆☆

## 🤯 Problem

You want to understand what Express is actually doing underneath.

Before frameworks existed,

how did people even build a server in Node.js?

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine building furniture.

`http` module = raw wood, nails, hand tools.

You CAN build a chair.

But it's slow and manual for every single piece.

Express = a furniture factory with pre-built parts.

---

## ✅ What is it?

Node's built-in module to create a web server

WITHOUT any external framework.

`http.createServer((req, res) => {})`

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

To understand what's happening under the hood.

Rarely used directly in real projects

once you know Express —

but it explains WHY Express exists.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🪑 Raw Wood vs Furniture Factory

http module = raw materials.

Express = the factory that makes building fast.

---

## 💼 Real Project

Almost no real production School ERP backend

is written using the raw `http` module alone —

manually parsing URLs, methods, and bodies for every route

gets messy FAST.

This is exactly the pain that leads every team

to reach for Express.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Saying "Express IS Node.js".

Express is a FRAMEWORK built ON TOP of the `http` module.

Node.js is the runtime; Express is a library that runs inside it.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```js
const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end('Hello World');
});

server.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'));
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

There's no direct Angular equivalent —

this is the raw HTTP handling layer

that a framework like Express normally hides from you,

similar to how Angular hides raw DOM manipulation from you.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

The http module is Node's built-in core module for creating web servers without any external framework. It gives low-level control over requests and responses, but requires manually handling routing, headers, and parsing — which is exactly the boilerplate that frameworks like Express are built to simplify.

---

## 📝 Keywords

http module

createServer

Core Module

Low-level

