# express.json() (Body Parsing) ⭐⭐⭐⭐☆

## 🤯 Problem

Frontend sends a POST request with `{ name: "Vaibhav", age: 25 }`.

In your route, you check `req.body`.

It's `undefined`.

The data is clearly THERE in the request — so why can't you read it?

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine a Sealed Envelope arriving at the office.

You can SEE the envelope exists (the request arrived).

But you can't read the letter inside

until someone OPENS it for you.

`express.json()` is the person who opens the envelope

and hands you the readable letter (`req.body`).

---

## ✅ What is it?

Built-in Express middleware that parses

incoming JSON request bodies

and makes the data available as `req.body`.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

Without it, `req.body` stays `undefined`

for any request sending JSON data —

meaning you can never read what the frontend actually sent.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

✉ The Envelope Opener

No `express.json()` = sealed envelope, unreadable.

With `express.json()` = envelope opened, `req.body` readable.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

Signup form sends `{ name, email, password }` as JSON.

`app.use(express.json())` unlocks that data

so the `/signup` route can actually read and save it.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Placing `express.json()` AFTER the routes that need `req.body`.

Middleware order matters —

it must be registered BEFORE any route that reads `req.body`.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```js
app.use(express.json()); // must be added BEFORE routes that need req.body

app.post('/users', (req, res) => {
    console.log(req.body); // { name: "Vaibhav", age: 25 }
    res.send('User received');
});
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Similar to how Angular's `HttpClient`

automatically serializes an object into JSON

when you call `http.post(url, data)`.

`express.json()` is the reverse — it DEserializes that JSON

back into a usable object on the server.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

express.json() is built-in middleware in Express that parses incoming requests with a JSON payload and populates req.body with the parsed object. Without registering this middleware before your routes, req.body would remain undefined for JSON requests.

