# REST API / CRUD Pattern ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

You've learned routes, middleware, and body parsing separately.

Now — how do they all fit together

to build one COMPLETE working API?

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine all the School ERP departments

(Reception, Security, Records Room)

working TOGETHER in one flow

to fully process a student admission request

— not just individually anymore.

---

## ✅ What is it?

REST API = a set of routes following CRUD:

Create, Read, Update, Delete —

mapped to POST, GET, PUT, DELETE.

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

It's the STANDARD pattern for building backend APIs

that any frontend (React, Angular, mobile app)

can consistently talk to.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

🔤 CRUD = Create, Read, Update, Delete

→ POST, GET, PUT, DELETE

Same chant as the Routing section — it all connects here.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP — Todo/Task Management for teachers

This EXACT pattern is reused for:

Students, Teachers, Fees, Attendance, Announcements —

literally every feature in the app.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Not sending correct HTTP status codes.

201 for Created, 204 for Deleted (no content), 404 for Not Found, 500 for Server Error —

sending 200 for everything is a common beginner mistake.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

let todos = [{ id: 1, task: "Learn Express" }];

app.get('/todos', (req, res) => res.json(todos));                      // Read all
app.post('/todos', (req, res) => {                                     // Create
    const todo = { id: Date.now(), task: req.body.task };
    todos.push(todo);
    res.status(201).json(todo);
});
app.put('/todos/:id', (req, res) => {                                  // Update
    const todo = todos.find(t => t.id == req.params.id);
    todo.task = req.body.task;
    res.json(todo);
});
app.delete('/todos/:id', (req, res) => {                               // Delete
    todos = todos.filter(t => t.id != req.params.id);
    res.status(204).send();
});

app.listen(3000);
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

This is EXACTLY what Angular's `HttpClient`

(`http.get()`, `http.post()`, `http.put()`, `http.delete()`)

is calling on the other end —

Angular sends the request, Express is what answers it.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

A REST API exposes CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete) through standard HTTP methods (POST, GET, PUT, DELETE) and predictable URL patterns. This pattern is the backbone of almost every backend feature — from managing users to products to orders — making the API consistent and easy for any frontend to consume.

---

## 📝 Keywords

CRUD

REST API

Status Codes

Create Read Update Delete

---


# MongoDB + Mongoose ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## 🤯 Problem

Right now, your `todos` array lives in server memory.

Restart the server → ALL data is gone.

Real apps need data to survive restarts,

and be shared across multiple server instances.

---

## 🧠 Think Like This

Imagine the School's Records Room.

An in-memory array = sticky notes on your desk (lost if you leave).

MongoDB = the permanent Records Room

where files are stored safely, forever, until someone deletes them.

Mongoose is the Clerk who knows the EXACT filing format

and talks to the Records Room on your behalf.

---

## ✅ What is it?

MongoDB = a NoSQL database that stores data as JSON-like documents.

Mongoose = an ODM (Object Data Modeling) library

that connects Node/Express to MongoDB

using Schemas (data shape) and Models (the interface to query/save data).

---

## 🤔 Why do we use it?

To PERSIST data permanently, beyond server restarts.

Mongoose adds structure/validation

on top of MongoDB's flexible document format.

---

## 🧠 Never Forget This

📁 Schema = the Filing Format

Schema = defines what fields a "Student" document has.

Model = the Clerk who actually saves/finds/updates those documents for you.

---

## 💼 Real Project

School ERP

Every student signup is saved PERMANENTLY

in the `students` collection in MongoDB —

surviving server restarts, accessible from anywhere,

instead of vanishing the moment the server reboots.

---

## ⚠ Interview Trap

❌ Forgetting `await` on Mongoose queries.

`User.create(...)` returns a Promise —

without `await`, you get a Promise object, NOT the actual saved data.

---

## 💻 Pattern

```js
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
mongoose.connect('mongodb://localhost:27017/myapp');

const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
    name: String,
    email: String
});
const User = mongoose.model('User', userSchema);

app.post('/users', async (req, res) => {
    const user = await User.create(req.body); // saves to MongoDB
    res.status(201).json(user);
});
```

---

## 🔄 Angular Brain

Think of a Mongoose Schema

like a TypeScript interface for your data model in Angular —

it defines the exact SHAPE the data must follow,

except Mongoose also enforces and validates it at the database level.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

Mongoose is an ODM library that provides a schema-based solution to model application data and interact with MongoDB. A Schema defines the structure and validation rules for documents, and a Model built from that schema is used to create, read, update, and delete documents in the database, replacing fragile in-memory data storage with persistent storage.

---

## 📝 Keywords

MongoDB

Mongoose

Schema

Model

ODM

Persistence

